WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.576 --> 00:11.036 align:middle
Transcript of Chairman Powell's
Press Conference January 30,

00:11.036 --> 00:11.846 align:middle
2019 CHAIRMAN POWELL.

00:11.846 --> 00:15.876 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone,
and welcome.

00:15.876 --> 00:18.286 align:middle
I will start with a
recap of our discussions,

00:18.916 --> 00:21.186 align:middle
including our assessment of
the outlook for the economy

00:21.536 --> 00:25.636 align:middle
and the judgments we made
about our interest rate policy

00:25.636 --> 00:26.506 align:middle
and our balance sheet.

00:27.166 --> 00:30.416 align:middle
I will cover the decisions
we made today as well

00:30.416 --> 00:33.296 align:middle
as our ongoing discussions
of matters on which we expect

00:33.296 --> 00:35.196 align:middle
to make decisions
in coming meetings.

00:36.596 --> 00:39.156 align:middle
My colleagues and I have
one overarching goal:

00:39.256 --> 00:41.136 align:middle
to sustain the economic
expansion,

00:41.836 --> 00:45.226 align:middle
with a strong job market and
stable prices, for the benefit

00:45.226 --> 00:46.076 align:middle
of the American people.

00:46.416 --> 00:50.866 align:middle
The U.S. economy is in a good
place, and we will continue

00:50.866 --> 00:53.786 align:middle
to use our monetary policy
tools to help keep it there.

00:55.406 --> 00:57.336 align:middle
The jobs picture
continues to be strong,

00:57.646 --> 00:59.866 align:middle
with the unemployment
rate near historic lows

01:00.426 --> 01:01.866 align:middle
and with stronger wage gains.

01:02.946 --> 01:05.296 align:middle
Inflation remains near
our 2 percent goal.

01:06.436 --> 01:09.636 align:middle
We continue to expect that
the American economy will grow

01:09.746 --> 01:13.656 align:middle
at a solid pace in 2019,
although likely slower

01:13.656 --> 01:15.896 align:middle
than the very strong
pace of 2018.

01:16.806 --> 01:20.046 align:middle
We believe that our current
policy stance is appropriate

01:20.046 --> 01:20.896 align:middle
at this time.

01:22.906 --> 01:24.246 align:middle
Despite this positive outlook,

01:24.466 --> 01:27.456 align:middle
over the past few months we
have seen some crosscurrents

01:27.456 --> 01:29.416 align:middle
and conflicting signals
about the outlook.

01:31.226 --> 01:34.116 align:middle
Growth has slowed in some
major foreign economies,

01:34.116 --> 01:35.836 align:middle
particularly in China
and Europe.

01:37.226 --> 01:38.676 align:middle
There is elevated uncertainty

01:38.676 --> 01:41.566 align:middle
around several unresolved
government policy issues,

01:41.666 --> 01:45.706 align:middle
including Brexit, ongoing trade
negotiations, and the effects

01:45.706 --> 01:48.176 align:middle
from the partial government
shutdown in the United States.

01:49.396 --> 01:52.616 align:middle
Financial conditions tightened
considerably late in 2018

01:53.146 --> 01:54.646 align:middle
and remain less supportive
of growth

01:54.706 --> 01:56.726 align:middle
than they were earlier in 2018.

01:57.856 --> 02:00.526 align:middle
And, while most of the incoming
domestic economic data have been

02:00.526 --> 02:02.746 align:middle
solid, some surveys of business

02:02.746 --> 02:04.386 align:middle
and consumer sentiment
have moved lower,

02:04.936 --> 02:05.996 align:middle
giving reason for caution.

02:07.556 --> 02:10.666 align:middle
We always emphasize that our
policies are data dependent.

02:11.446 --> 02:13.296 align:middle
In other words, as
economic conditions

02:13.296 --> 02:16.676 align:middle
and the outlook evolve, we
take that new information

02:16.726 --> 02:18.526 align:middle
into account in setting
our policies.

02:19.876 --> 02:21.916 align:middle
We are now facing a somewhat
contradictory picture

02:21.916 --> 02:24.956 align:middle
of generally strong U.S.
macroeconomic performance

02:25.486 --> 02:27.666 align:middle
alongside growing
evidence of crosscurrents.

02:28.426 --> 02:32.416 align:middle
At such times, common-sense risk
management suggests patiently

02:32.416 --> 02:35.116 align:middle
awaiting greater
clarity-an approach

02:35.116 --> 02:37.706 align:middle
that has served policymakers
well in the past.

02:38.796 --> 02:40.176 align:middle
With that in mind,
I'd like to spell

02:40.176 --> 02:42.426 align:middle
out how the Federal Open Market
Committee has been thinking

02:42.426 --> 02:43.396 align:middle
about these issues.

02:45.156 --> 02:47.586 align:middle
At our December meeting,
we noted the solid outlook

02:47.586 --> 02:50.486 align:middle
for steady growth, vigorous job
creation, and price stability.

02:50.736 --> 02:52.906 align:middle
We also stressed that
the extent and timing

02:52.906 --> 02:55.696 align:middle
of any rate increases were
uncertain and would depend

02:55.696 --> 02:57.496 align:middle
on incoming data and
the evolving outlook.

02:58.596 --> 03:01.216 align:middle
We therefore said that we
would be paying close attention

03:01.216 --> 03:03.486 align:middle
to global economic and
financial developments

03:04.006 --> 03:06.526 align:middle
and assessing their implications
for the economic outlook.

03:07.896 --> 03:11.056 align:middle
Today the FOMC decided
that the cumulative effects

03:11.056 --> 03:14.116 align:middle
of those developments over the
last several months warrant a

03:14.166 --> 03:17.856 align:middle
patient, wait-and-see approach
regarding future policy changes.

03:18.606 --> 03:20.676 align:middle
In particular, our
statement today says,

03:21.256 --> 03:23.646 align:middle
"In light of global economic
and financial developments

03:23.646 --> 03:26.526 align:middle
and muted inflation pressures,
the Committee will be patient

03:26.966 --> 03:30.566 align:middle
as it determines what future
adjustments to the target range

03:30.956 --> 03:33.206 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate may be appropriate."

03:35.086 --> 03:37.166 align:middle
This change was not
driven by a major shift

03:37.226 --> 03:39.176 align:middle
in the baseline outlook
for the economy.

03:39.696 --> 03:43.216 align:middle
Like many forecasters, we
still see "sustained expansion

03:43.216 --> 03:44.386 align:middle
of economic activity,

03:44.646 --> 03:47.406 align:middle
strong labor market
conditions, and inflation near .

03:47.406 --> 03:49.656 align:middle
. . 2 percent" as
the likeliest case.

03:50.186 --> 03:52.746 align:middle
But the crosscurrents I
mentioned suggest the risk

03:52.746 --> 03:54.076 align:middle
of a less favorable outlook.

03:55.996 --> 03:57.416 align:middle
In addition, the case

03:57.416 --> 03:59.346 align:middle
for raising rates has
weakened somewhat.

04:00.706 --> 04:02.736 align:middle
The traditional case
for rate increases is

04:02.736 --> 04:04.966 align:middle
to protect the economy
from risks that arise

04:04.966 --> 04:09.086 align:middle
when rates are too low for too
long, particularly the risk

04:09.086 --> 04:10.236 align:middle
of too-high inflation.

04:11.686 --> 04:14.376 align:middle
Over the past few months, that
risk appears to have diminished.

04:15.136 --> 04:18.076 align:middle
Inflation readings have been
muted, and the recent drop

04:18.076 --> 04:21.756 align:middle
in oil prices is likely to push
headline inflation lower still

04:21.856 --> 04:22.676 align:middle
in coming months.

04:23.666 --> 04:26.136 align:middle
Further, as we noted in
our postmeeting statement,

04:26.876 --> 04:28.966 align:middle
while survey-based measures

04:28.966 --> 04:31.286 align:middle
of inflation expectations
have been stable,

04:32.036 --> 04:33.456 align:middle
financial market measures

04:33.456 --> 04:35.526 align:middle
of inflation compensation
have moved lower.

04:36.846 --> 04:40.276 align:middle
Similarly, the balance-the risk
of financial imbalances appears

04:40.276 --> 04:42.656 align:middle
to have receded, as a
number of indicators

04:42.656 --> 04:44.046 align:middle
that showed elevated levels

04:44.046 --> 04:47.646 align:middle
of financial risk appetite
last fall have moved closer

04:47.646 --> 04:48.886 align:middle
to historical norms.

04:50.076 --> 04:53.136 align:middle
In this environment, we believe
we can best support the economy

04:53.606 --> 04:56.676 align:middle
by being patient in evaluating
the outlook before making any

04:56.676 --> 04:58.186 align:middle
future adjustment to policy.

05:00.176 --> 05:02.376 align:middle
Let me now turn to balance
sheet normalization.

05:04.286 --> 05:05.786 align:middle
Over its past three meetings,

05:05.786 --> 05:08.126 align:middle
the FOMC has held
in-depth discussions

05:08.126 --> 05:10.146 align:middle
on the final stages
of this process.

05:10.426 --> 05:12.096 align:middle
Today we made some
important progress

05:12.096 --> 05:14.986 align:middle
in clarifying the path
forward, as summarized

05:14.986 --> 05:17.636 align:middle
in the Statement Regarding
Monetary Policy Implementation

05:18.006 --> 05:20.486 align:middle
and Balance Sheet
Normalization that we released

05:20.486 --> 05:22.086 align:middle
with today's FOMC statement.

05:23.316 --> 05:25.576 align:middle
The Committee made the
fundamental decision today

05:25.576 --> 05:28.916 align:middle
to continue indefinitely using
our current operating procedure

05:28.916 --> 05:30.566 align:middle
for implementing
monetary policy.

05:31.366 --> 05:34.346 align:middle
That is, we will continue to
use our administered rates

05:34.346 --> 05:37.926 align:middle
to control the policy rate with
an ample supply of reserves

05:38.186 --> 05:41.096 align:middle
so that active management
of reserves is not required.

05:41.866 --> 05:43.696 align:middle
This is often called
a "floor system"

05:43.896 --> 05:45.486 align:middle
or an "abundant reserves
system."

05:46.886 --> 05:49.056 align:middle
Under the current set
of operating procedures,

05:49.056 --> 05:49.696 align:middle
as outlined

05:49.696 --> 05:51.766 align:middle
in the implementation
note released today,

05:52.246 --> 05:53.956 align:middle
this means that the
federal funds rate,

05:54.036 --> 05:57.426 align:middle
our active policy tool, is
held within its target range

05:57.846 --> 06:00.646 align:middle
by appropriately setting the
Federal Reserve's administered

06:00.646 --> 06:03.716 align:middle
rates of interest on reserves,
as well as the offer rate

06:03.716 --> 06:06.146 align:middle
on the overnight
reverse repo facility,

06:06.496 --> 06:09.656 align:middle
without managing the
supply of reserves actively.

06:09.946 --> 06:13.056 align:middle
As the minutes of our recent
discussions have indicated,

06:13.316 --> 06:14.906 align:middle
the FOMC strongly believes

06:14.936 --> 06:16.866 align:middle
that this approach
provides good control

06:16.866 --> 06:19.486 align:middle
of short-term money
market rates in a variety

06:19.486 --> 06:22.126 align:middle
of market conditions and
effective transmission

06:22.126 --> 06:24.306 align:middle
of those rates to broader
financial conditions.

06:26.416 --> 06:29.386 align:middle
Settling this central question
clears the way for the FOMC

06:29.386 --> 06:31.776 align:middle
to address a number of
further questions regarding the

06:31.776 --> 06:33.976 align:middle
remaining stages of
balance sheet normalization.

06:34.996 --> 06:37.506 align:middle
The decision to retain our
current operating procedure

06:37.506 --> 06:40.366 align:middle
means that, after allowing
for currency in circulation,

06:40.916 --> 06:43.666 align:middle
the ultimate size of our balance
sheet will be driven principally

06:44.006 --> 06:46.526 align:middle
by financial institutions'
demand for reserves,

06:47.046 --> 06:49.296 align:middle
plus a buffer so
that fluctuations

06:49.296 --> 06:52.256 align:middle
in reserve demand
do not require us

06:52.256 --> 06:54.816 align:middle
to make frequent sizable
market interventions.

06:56.146 --> 06:57.156 align:middle
Estimates of the level

06:57.156 --> 06:58.916 align:middle
of reserve demand
are quite uncertain,

06:59.636 --> 07:00.906 align:middle
but we know that this demand

07:00.986 --> 07:04.376 align:middle
in the post-crisis environment
is far larger than before.

07:05.286 --> 07:07.486 align:middle
Higher reserve holdings
are an important part

07:07.526 --> 07:09.266 align:middle
of the stronger liquidity
position

07:09.366 --> 07:11.436 align:middle
that financial institutions
must now hold.

07:12.506 --> 07:15.866 align:middle
Moreover, based on surveys
and market intelligence,

07:16.036 --> 07:18.866 align:middle
current estimates of reserve
demand are considerably higher

07:18.866 --> 07:20.576 align:middle
than estimates of
a year or so ago.

07:21.496 --> 07:23.946 align:middle
The implication is that the
normalization of the size

07:23.946 --> 07:26.096 align:middle
of the portfolio will
be completed sooner,

07:26.666 --> 07:30.516 align:middle
and with a larger balance sheet,
than in previous estimates.

07:32.106 --> 07:33.236 align:middle
In light of these estimates

07:33.236 --> 07:36.066 align:middle
and the substantial progress we
have made in reducing reserves,

07:36.166 --> 07:38.826 align:middle
the Committee is now evaluating
the appropriate timing

07:39.266 --> 07:40.966 align:middle
for the end of balance
sheet runoff.

07:41.986 --> 07:44.266 align:middle
This decision will
likely be part of a plan

07:44.266 --> 07:47.056 align:middle
for gradually reaching our
ultimate balance sheet goals

07:47.496 --> 07:48.596 align:middle
while minimizing risks

07:48.596 --> 07:50.636 align:middle
to achieving our
dual-mandate objectives

07:50.926 --> 07:52.956 align:middle
and avoiding unnecessary
market disruption.

07:53.946 --> 07:56.726 align:middle
We will be finalizing these
plans at coming meetings.

07:57.316 --> 08:01.576 align:middle
The process of balance sheet
normalization is unprecedented.

08:01.856 --> 08:03.656 align:middle
Throughout this process
we've attempted to lay

08:03.656 --> 08:06.296 align:middle
out our plans well in
advance, and we've been willing

08:06.296 --> 08:08.966 align:middle
to make changes as we learn
more about the process.

08:10.206 --> 08:11.006 align:middle
The implementation

08:11.006 --> 08:13.416 align:middle
and normalization statement
released today is intended

08:13.416 --> 08:16.186 align:middle
to provide some additional
clarity regarding the conditions

08:16.186 --> 08:18.146 align:middle
under which we might
adjust our plans.

08:18.806 --> 08:21.576 align:middle
The statement makes
three points: First,

08:22.126 --> 08:23.356 align:middle
as we've long emphasized,

08:23.506 --> 08:26.486 align:middle
the federal funds rate is our
active monetary policy tool.

08:27.416 --> 08:29.856 align:middle
Second, as far as the
particular details

08:29.856 --> 08:32.676 align:middle
of normalization are
concerned, we will not hesitate

08:32.676 --> 08:34.836 align:middle
to make changes in
light of economic

08:34.836 --> 08:35.936 align:middle
and financial developments.

08:37.656 --> 08:40.476 align:middle
This does not mean that we
would use the balance sheet

08:40.476 --> 08:41.456 align:middle
as an active tool,

08:42.426 --> 08:44.716 align:middle
but occasional changes
could be warranted.

08:45.566 --> 08:47.686 align:middle
Third, we repeat a sentence

08:48.206 --> 08:51.836 align:middle
of the normalization principles
we adopted in June of 2017.

08:52.696 --> 08:55.466 align:middle
While the federal funds rate
would remain our active tool

08:55.466 --> 08:58.636 align:middle
of policy in a wide range
of scenarios, we recognize

08:58.636 --> 09:00.876 align:middle
that the economy could
again present conditions

09:01.136 --> 09:04.116 align:middle
in which federal funds rate
policy is not sufficient.

09:05.136 --> 09:07.446 align:middle
In those cases, the
FOMC would be prepared

09:07.446 --> 09:10.646 align:middle
to use its full range of tools,
including balance sheet policy.

09:13.156 --> 09:15.836 align:middle
Times of economic uncertainty
put a premium on the clarity

09:15.836 --> 09:17.876 align:middle
and predictability
of FOMC policy.

09:18.076 --> 09:20.376 align:middle
We are committed to clearly
explaining what we are doing

09:20.376 --> 09:23.416 align:middle
and why we're doing it, both
regarding the path of rates

09:23.416 --> 09:25.536 align:middle
and also regarding management
of the balance sheet.

09:26.266 --> 09:28.796 align:middle
We believe that this
transparency is how we can best

09:28.796 --> 09:30.606 align:middle
contribute to macroeconomic
stability.

09:31.656 --> 09:32.236 align:middle
Thank you.

09:32.236 --> 09:33.866 align:middle
I'll be glad to take
your questions.

09:34.776 --> 09:36.196 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:37.256 --> 09:38.956 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

09:39.306 --> 09:41.246 align:middle
So you've said many
times recently

09:41.246 --> 09:42.996 align:middle
that policy was still
accommodative

09:43.066 --> 09:45.286 align:middle
and that it needn't be so.

09:45.286 --> 09:47.906 align:middle
You've said recently
that policy-the state

09:47.906 --> 09:49.566 align:middle
of the policy was
still accommodative

09:49.726 --> 09:52.066 align:middle
and that the economy
didn't require that anymore.

09:52.926 --> 09:53.986 align:middle
Is that still the case?

09:53.986 --> 09:57.976 align:middle
If so, how do you justify
the removal of the need

09:58.076 --> 10:00.186 align:middle
for some further
interest rate increases?

10:01.436 --> 10:03.006 align:middle
Basically, are we
at neutral now,

10:03.006 --> 10:05.626 align:middle
or does the economy
still need accommodation?

10:06.506 --> 10:07.256 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

10:07.256 --> 10:11.666 align:middle
We think that our policy is at
the appropriate point right now.

10:11.666 --> 10:13.626 align:middle
We think our policy stance
is appropriate right now.

10:13.706 --> 10:19.446 align:middle
We do. We also know that our
policy rate is now in the range

10:19.746 --> 10:21.486 align:middle
of the Committee's
estimates of neutral.

10:22.276 --> 10:26.756 align:middle
So we'll be-again, we think our
policy stance is appropriate.

10:28.216 --> 10:29.026 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

10:31.766 --> 10:33.336 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

10:33.866 --> 10:37.496 align:middle
Last week, the IMF said
risks are clearly skewed

10:37.496 --> 10:40.536 align:middle
to the downside for the
U.S. and global economy.

10:40.786 --> 10:44.656 align:middle
Can you clarify-does the
FOMC see risks as skewed

10:44.656 --> 10:48.016 align:middle
to the downside, particularly
after you removed the statement

10:48.016 --> 10:49.646 align:middle
about risks being balanced?

10:49.646 --> 10:49.956 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

10:50.536 --> 10:54.716 align:middle
We had an extensive discussion
of the baseline and also

10:54.716 --> 10:58.256 align:middle
of the risks to the baseline,
and the risks are, of course,

10:58.776 --> 11:00.726 align:middle
the fact that financial
conditions have tightened,

11:01.256 --> 11:05.966 align:middle
that global growth has slowed,
as well as some, let's say,

11:05.966 --> 11:09.966 align:middle
government-related risks like
Brexit and trade discussions,

11:09.966 --> 11:11.996 align:middle
and also the effects

11:12.476 --> 11:14.036 align:middle
and ultimate disposition
of the shutdown.

11:14.566 --> 11:17.296 align:middle
So we looked at-we look at
those, and the way we think

11:17.296 --> 11:20.936 align:middle
of it is that policy-we will
use our policy, and we have,

11:21.006 --> 11:23.026 align:middle
to offset risks to the baseline.

11:23.536 --> 11:28.066 align:middle
So we view the baseline
as still solid,

11:28.066 --> 11:31.496 align:middle
and part of that is the way
we adjusted our baseline

11:31.496 --> 11:32.686 align:middle
to address those risks.

11:33.036 --> 11:34.546 align:middle
So that's the way we're
thinking about that now.

11:35.166 --> 11:36.856 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

11:37.836 --> 11:41.896 align:middle
Thanks. Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

11:41.896 --> 11:45.036 align:middle
The Fed has linked its
commitment to patience in part

11:45.036 --> 11:46.996 align:middle
to subdued inflation outcomes.

11:46.996 --> 11:49.496 align:middle
Would you be comfortable
with continuing patience even

11:49.496 --> 11:51.706 align:middle
if there are modest,
if transient,

11:51.706 --> 11:55.516 align:middle
overshoots to the inflation
target by core inflation?

11:55.516 --> 11:57.986 align:middle
How sensitive are you
right now to developments

11:57.986 --> 12:00.306 align:middle
in core inflation when
determining the next move?

12:01.146 --> 12:02.536 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

12:02.536 --> 12:07.936 align:middle
You know, generally speaking, we
think the outlook is favorable,

12:07.986 --> 12:09.936 align:middle
and we think that
these crosscurrents

12:09.936 --> 12:11.406 align:middle
that I referred to-these
risks-are going

12:11.406 --> 12:12.316 align:middle
to be with us for a while.

12:12.916 --> 12:14.336 align:middle
We think our stance
is appropriate.

12:14.506 --> 12:17.256 align:middle
We think there's no pressing
need to change our policy stance

12:17.376 --> 12:18.896 align:middle
and no need to rush
to judgment there.

12:19.636 --> 12:23.796 align:middle
I would say that we'll be
looking at a full range of data,

12:24.256 --> 12:26.596 align:middle
and that will include
inflation data.

12:26.596 --> 12:29.836 align:middle
It will include all data
relevant to our dual mandate

12:30.006 --> 12:31.796 align:middle
of stable prices and
maximum employment.

12:32.376 --> 12:35.746 align:middle
Remember that our inflation
objective is a symmetric one,

12:36.376 --> 12:39.576 align:middle
meaning that we are-we're always
trying to get to 2 percent,

12:40.246 --> 12:44.776 align:middle
and we don't look at-we look at
inflation equally on both sides.

12:44.776 --> 12:49.296 align:middle
So I can't get into specific
hypotheticals, but I do believe

12:49.296 --> 12:50.856 align:middle
that we have a symmetric
objective,

12:50.856 --> 12:54.506 align:middle
and I believe we'll
act that way.

12:54.776 --> 12:55.596 align:middle
RICHARD MILLER.

12:56.216 --> 13:00.686 align:middle
We learned from the last
minutes of the meeting that some

13:01.626 --> 13:02.906 align:middle
of the tweaks you make

13:02.906 --> 13:07.126 align:middle
to the statement have a lot
more portent than we thought.

13:07.666 --> 13:09.606 align:middle
In light of that, I'm wondering
if you can provide us with a

13:09.606 --> 13:12.176 align:middle
"decoder ring" for
this statement.

13:12.536 --> 13:16.306 align:middle
"Adjustments"-does that
suggest the next move

13:16.306 --> 13:19.086 align:middle
in interest rates is as
likely to be up as down?

13:19.686 --> 13:23.306 align:middle
And when you twin "in
light of global economic

13:23.306 --> 13:25.776 align:middle
and financial developments" with
"muted inflation pressures,"

13:26.186 --> 13:29.046 align:middle
does that suggest you have
to see both the cessation

13:29.046 --> 13:30.936 align:middle
of these crosscurrents
that you've talked about

13:31.906 --> 13:36.736 align:middle
and inflation moving up
before you get off the dime,

13:37.126 --> 13:38.176 align:middle
so to speak, and move?

13:38.176 --> 13:39.686 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

13:39.876 --> 13:41.336 align:middle
Sorry-say again what
the first question was?

13:41.336 --> 13:41.706 align:middle
RICHARD MILLER.

13:41.776 --> 13:43.706 align:middle
First question is,
"adjustments"-is that meant

13:43.706 --> 13:47.076 align:middle
to imply the next move is as
most likely to be up as down?

13:47.076 --> 13:47.596 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

13:47.756 --> 13:50.466 align:middle
Yes. I'm just going to
say that the-it's going

13:50.466 --> 13:51.926 align:middle
to depend entirely on the data.

13:52.056 --> 13:53.246 align:middle
We're not making a judgment.

13:53.246 --> 13:54.396 align:middle
We don't have a strong prior.

13:54.966 --> 14:00.486 align:middle
We will be-we will patiently
wait and let the data clarify.

14:01.086 --> 14:05.326 align:middle
Some of the crosscurrents
that I refer to may be with us

14:05.326 --> 14:10.036 align:middle
for a while, and I think we'll
be looking at seeing those clear

14:10.036 --> 14:13.046 align:middle
up as it relates to-as
they relate to the outlook

14:13.046 --> 14:16.546 align:middle
for the U.S. and that will be
an important aspect as well.

14:17.016 --> 14:18.256 align:middle
Your second question was?

14:18.786 --> 14:19.196 align:middle
RICHARD MILLER.

14:19.726 --> 14:21.376 align:middle
Would it be not just
the cessation

14:21.376 --> 14:24.616 align:middle
of those crosscurrents,
but the end

14:24.616 --> 14:29.096 align:middle
of these muted inflation
pressures-are they both sort

14:29.096 --> 14:31.456 align:middle
of things that are
necessary for you

14:31.856 --> 14:32.976 align:middle
to change this "patient" stance?

14:32.976 --> 14:33.396 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

14:33.396 --> 14:36.726 align:middle
You know, it's really hard
to speak about generalities.

14:36.726 --> 14:40.806 align:middle
We'll be looking at everything,
but I do think that, you know,

14:40.806 --> 14:44.826 align:middle
muted inflation pressures-you
know,

14:44.826 --> 14:48.156 align:middle
you would want-I would
want to see a need

14:48.156 --> 14:50.446 align:middle
for further rate
increases, and, for me,

14:50.446 --> 14:52.176 align:middle
a big part of that
would be inflation.

14:52.826 --> 14:54.536 align:middle
It wouldn't be the only thing,

14:54.536 --> 14:55.656 align:middle
but it would certainly
be important.

14:56.836 --> 14:59.366 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:59.446 --> 15:00.996 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

15:01.296 --> 15:04.816 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, did the Committee
discuss an actual change

15:05.246 --> 15:10.546 align:middle
to the runoff policy or the
runoff schedule right now?

15:10.546 --> 15:13.696 align:middle
If so, is that under
consideration right now,

15:13.696 --> 15:14.566 align:middle
and when might we know?

15:15.006 --> 15:17.506 align:middle
The second thing is, I have
to nail down this thing.

15:17.796 --> 15:22.676 align:middle
You guys, Fed folks, keep
mentioning the "market average"

15:22.676 --> 15:25.446 align:middle
or the "market outlook" for
the size of the balance sheet.

15:26.266 --> 15:29.996 align:middle
Are you endorsing the market
average, which is 3½ trillion?

15:30.326 --> 15:33.696 align:middle
And if you're not endorsing it,
why do you keep mentioning it?

15:34.366 --> 15:36.446 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

15:37.176 --> 15:39.816 align:middle
Okay. So today I'm here
to talk about decisions

15:39.816 --> 15:41.476 align:middle
and also discussions
about decisions

15:41.476 --> 15:42.316 align:middle
that haven't been made.

15:42.316 --> 15:44.476 align:middle
So we're talking about
the latter thing,

15:44.476 --> 15:46.326 align:middle
which is discussions,
and so I can't get ahead

15:46.326 --> 15:47.366 align:middle
of where decisions are.

15:47.976 --> 15:50.066 align:middle
But so the Committee
is-what we're looking

15:50.066 --> 15:56.306 align:middle
to do is create a whole plan
that will bring us to our goal,

15:56.356 --> 15:58.816 align:middle
our longer-run goal, which
is a balance sheet no larger

15:58.816 --> 16:02.946 align:middle
than it needs to be for us to
efficiently conduct-efficiently

16:02.946 --> 16:06.256 align:middle
and effectively conduct monetary
policy, but to do so in a way

16:06.256 --> 16:08.066 align:middle
that doesn't put our
goals at risk or result

16:08.066 --> 16:09.596 align:middle
in unnecessary market turmoil.

16:09.596 --> 16:12.696 align:middle
So there are a lot of pieces
to that, and we've learned

16:12.696 --> 16:16.646 align:middle
over time that it's-when making
these-when designing these

16:16.676 --> 16:19.556 align:middle
plans, like, for example, the
original normalization plan,

16:19.556 --> 16:20.856 align:middle
it's good to take your time.

16:21.126 --> 16:23.596 align:middle
Let the best ideas
rise to the top.

16:23.916 --> 16:27.216 align:middle
Let them stand the test of
time and argument and then move

16:27.216 --> 16:29.446 align:middle
when you're really
comfortable with what you've got

16:29.446 --> 16:31.656 align:middle
and when you feel you can
communicate it clearly.

16:31.656 --> 16:34.676 align:middle
So I don't want to get
ahead of that process today.

16:34.676 --> 16:38.436 align:middle
But-so we've discussed-there
are a number

16:38.436 --> 16:40.166 align:middle
of pieces to that puzzle.

16:40.166 --> 16:41.976 align:middle
There are several
different pieces to them,

16:41.976 --> 16:43.366 align:middle
and I think they're
coming-and I'm very pleased

16:43.366 --> 16:46.716 align:middle
with the progress that
we've made, and, you know,

16:46.716 --> 16:48.256 align:middle
the piece that you
mentioned is something

16:48.256 --> 16:49.806 align:middle
that is in those discussions.

16:49.806 --> 16:50.786 align:middle
That's the first question.

16:51.426 --> 16:54.006 align:middle
I'm not going to
give our estimate

16:54.006 --> 16:55.736 align:middle
or ratify anybody
else's estimate

16:55.736 --> 16:58.116 align:middle
of what the equilibrium
balance sheet is here today.

16:58.116 --> 17:01.426 align:middle
There are estimates out there,
but I'm not at a point today

17:01.426 --> 17:03.486 align:middle
where I'm going to be
giving out numbers on that.

17:04.046 --> 17:07.016 align:middle
But there are estimates, and
I think they're consistent

17:07.016 --> 17:08.616 align:middle
with what I said,
broadly speaking.

17:09.686 --> 17:10.046 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:10.046 --> 17:11.326 align:middle
So, just to clarify-I'm sorry,

17:12.006 --> 17:15.266 align:middle
you did discuss reducing
the pace of the runoff?

17:15.646 --> 17:18.406 align:middle
That sounds like
what you said, to me.

17:18.406 --> 17:18.806 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

17:18.806 --> 17:21.086 align:middle
Yes, it's-so, again, we
haven't made any decisions.

17:21.086 --> 17:22.476 align:middle
There no decisions
have been made.

17:22.676 --> 17:25.806 align:middle
There are different
pieces of the thing,

17:25.806 --> 17:28.196 align:middle
and that is in the discussion
as one of many pieces

17:28.196 --> 17:29.096 align:middle
that is in the discussion.

17:29.096 --> 17:30.906 align:middle
And that was reflected in
the last minutes, actually.

17:31.186 --> 17:32.286 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:32.596 --> 17:36.806 align:middle
Thanks. Nick Timiraos of
the Wall Street Journal.

17:37.106 --> 17:39.836 align:middle
Chairman Powell, I want to
follow up on Steve's question.

17:40.006 --> 17:42.586 align:middle
The size of the balance sheet
obviously matters a great deal,

17:42.816 --> 17:45.776 align:middle
but, so, too, if you follow
the arguments that were made

17:45.776 --> 17:47.696 align:middle
when the Fed was
purchasing these assets,

17:47.796 --> 17:49.896 align:middle
does the duration
of those holdings.

17:49.896 --> 17:54.206 align:middle
So once you are-once you reach
the terminal level of reserves

17:54.206 --> 17:57.376 align:middle
and you have to reinvest
maturing mortgage-backed

17:57.376 --> 18:01.046 align:middle
securities into Treasuries,
it will matter a great deal,

18:01.046 --> 18:03.726 align:middle
of course, where along
the curve the Fed resumes

18:03.726 --> 18:04.536 align:middle
those reinvestments.

18:04.536 --> 18:08.186 align:middle
And so I wonder, what does
your staff research show

18:08.186 --> 18:09.876 align:middle
about the degree
of accommodation

18:09.876 --> 18:13.636 align:middle
that would be provided by
either moving to the front end

18:13.636 --> 18:15.826 align:middle
of the curve and holding
or investing, perhaps,

18:15.826 --> 18:19.656 align:middle
in shorter maturity assets,
versus the other approach

18:19.656 --> 18:22.586 align:middle
that was identified in the
December meeting minutes,

18:22.926 --> 18:27.036 align:middle
which would be to
simply reflect issuance

18:27.036 --> 18:29.466 align:middle
of the outstanding
Treasury maturities?

18:30.176 --> 18:32.836 align:middle
And which approach do you
think is more appropriate

18:33.076 --> 18:34.576 align:middle
in the current environment?

18:34.576 --> 18:35.356 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

18:35.356 --> 18:36.296 align:middle
These are great questions.

18:36.376 --> 18:39.846 align:middle
Let me say that the question
of the ultimate composition

18:39.846 --> 18:40.556 align:middle
of our balance sheet

18:40.556 --> 18:43.636 align:middle
in the longer run is a very
important one, and it's one

18:43.636 --> 18:47.916 align:middle
that we see ourselves as coming
to, you know, fairly soon,

18:48.066 --> 18:49.896 align:middle
as in, in coming meetings.

18:49.896 --> 18:52.766 align:middle
It won't be the first thing
we work on, but it will be one

18:52.766 --> 18:54.436 align:middle
of the first things
that we try to resolve.

18:54.436 --> 18:58.646 align:middle
We had discussions at one of
the last two meetings on this,

18:58.726 --> 19:01.376 align:middle
and we haven't come
to a judgment on that.

19:02.036 --> 19:04.056 align:middle
And, you know, I wouldn't
comment beyond saying

19:04.056 --> 19:08.296 align:middle
that we understand that's a
key question, and there are,

19:08.456 --> 19:11.006 align:middle
you know, there are
issues to be decided.

19:11.006 --> 19:12.416 align:middle
There are benefits and costs

19:12.416 --> 19:14.666 align:middle
to doing-to taking different
approaches, and I wouldn't want

19:14.666 --> 19:16.196 align:middle
to prejudge them today.

19:17.036 --> 19:17.976 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

19:19.336 --> 19:20.676 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum, New York Times.

19:21.126 --> 19:23.786 align:middle
I am struggling a little bit
to understand what has changed

19:23.786 --> 19:26.096 align:middle
since we sat here with
you six weeks ago.

19:26.096 --> 19:27.256 align:middle
You've said today that you think

19:27.256 --> 19:30.206 align:middle
that inflation would be the
reason that the Fed would need

19:30.206 --> 19:31.406 align:middle
to continue raising rates.

19:31.446 --> 19:33.996 align:middle
Has the inflation outlook
shifted that dramatically

19:33.996 --> 19:34.766 align:middle
in the last six weeks?

19:34.766 --> 19:37.886 align:middle
Can you speak specifically to
why you've moved from a posture

19:37.886 --> 19:40.186 align:middle
of saying we expect to keep
raising rates this year

19:40.566 --> 19:42.346 align:middle
to a posture of standing still?

19:42.986 --> 19:43.776 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

19:43.776 --> 19:45.316 align:middle
I'd point to a couple of things.

19:45.316 --> 19:51.026 align:middle
First, the narrative of
slowing global growth continues,

19:51.026 --> 19:51.476 align:middle
if you will.

19:51.476 --> 19:55.606 align:middle
The incoming data have
shown more of that.

19:55.606 --> 19:58.246 align:middle
We've seen that both in
China and in Western Europe,

19:58.686 --> 20:01.416 align:middle
and so that's an important-that
has important implications

20:01.416 --> 20:04.876 align:middle
for us, and that story
has-let's just say it continues.

20:05.046 --> 20:12.156 align:middle
And, in addition-I mean, I
think important-possibly less

20:12.156 --> 20:13.846 align:middle
important now-probably
less important now,

20:13.846 --> 20:16.616 align:middle
but-has been the shutdown,
which will leave some sort

20:16.616 --> 20:18.426 align:middle
of imprint on first-quarter GDP.

20:18.426 --> 20:21.286 align:middle
We don't know the
ultimate resolution of it.

20:21.696 --> 20:24.406 align:middle
If that's all there is,
and the shutdown is gone

20:24.406 --> 20:27.896 align:middle
and there isn't another
shutdown, then we'll get most

20:27.896 --> 20:31.376 align:middle
of that back in the second
period-second quarter.

20:31.726 --> 20:35.266 align:middle
So those things-in addition,
you know, you have to look back.

20:35.266 --> 20:37.856 align:middle
Financial conditions began to
tighten in the fourth quarter.

20:38.266 --> 20:40.236 align:middle
And they now have persisted

20:40.236 --> 20:43.086 align:middle
and remain tighter-significantly
tighter,

20:43.086 --> 20:45.556 align:middle
let's say-than they were, and
that's something that we have

20:45.556 --> 20:46.786 align:middle
to take into account as well.

20:47.476 --> 20:49.456 align:middle
So that's where we are.

20:51.676 --> 20:52.476 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

20:53.406 --> 20:56.806 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

20:56.806 --> 20:59.126 align:middle
You said you like to be a
plain speaker, so let me try

20:59.126 --> 21:01.506 align:middle
to have you put this
as plainly as possible.

21:01.876 --> 21:05.526 align:middle
Is it fair to characterize
this as not a pause

21:05.626 --> 21:07.886 align:middle
in a tightening cycle,
but the end

21:07.886 --> 21:12.106 align:middle
of the tightening cycle
now-a new regime for the Fed?

21:12.566 --> 21:15.506 align:middle
And, if not, can you put
a time frame on "patient"?

21:15.816 --> 21:17.816 align:middle
Does "patient" have
some sort of time frame?

21:18.406 --> 21:21.706 align:middle
And I'm wondering
about your reaction

21:21.706 --> 21:25.696 align:middle
to the criticism you got on your
December 19th news conference,

21:26.076 --> 21:28.676 align:middle
your later statements-do
you feel you've put a

21:28.676 --> 21:30.476 align:middle
"Powell put" into the markets?

21:31.326 --> 21:32.106 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

21:32.306 --> 21:35.736 align:middle
So, patience-I think we're
going to know in hindsight,

21:35.836 --> 21:41.456 align:middle
because the length of this
patient period is going

21:41.456 --> 21:43.906 align:middle
to depend entirely
on incoming data

21:43.906 --> 21:45.426 align:middle
and its implications
for the outlook.

21:45.576 --> 21:48.716 align:middle
So that's going to be-so
it's hard to say, you know,

21:48.716 --> 21:50.636 align:middle
it's hard to think
of what to call it

21:50.636 --> 21:54.046 align:middle
at this point or
how to label it.

21:54.046 --> 21:57.926 align:middle
So really, your second question
is about how do we think

21:57.926 --> 21:59.116 align:middle
about financial conditions.

21:59.836 --> 22:03.346 align:middle
And I would say this: We
think about a broad range

22:03.346 --> 22:06.016 align:middle
of financial conditions,
not just one or two things.

22:06.016 --> 22:09.536 align:middle
You know, it's interest
rates, it's risk spreads,

22:09.536 --> 22:12.896 align:middle
it's currency, it's
the stock market,

22:12.896 --> 22:15.496 align:middle
it's credit availability,
it's many, many factors.

22:15.826 --> 22:18.496 align:middle
And what matters for
financial conditions is,

22:18.566 --> 22:21.156 align:middle
when there are changes and
those changes are sustained

22:21.216 --> 22:24.306 align:middle
for a period of time, then
they become important for us

22:24.306 --> 22:26.636 align:middle
because they have important
macroeconomic implications.

22:27.006 --> 22:28.076 align:middle
So that's how we think about it.

22:28.436 --> 22:34.486 align:middle
So we don't react to, you know,
to most things that happen

22:34.546 --> 22:35.276 align:middle
in the financial markets.

22:35.276 --> 22:38.386 align:middle
But when we see a sustained
change in financial conditions,

22:38.426 --> 22:41.686 align:middle
then that's something that
has to play into our thinking.

22:41.996 --> 22:43.356 align:middle
In fact, our policy works

22:43.356 --> 22:45.576 align:middle
through changing financial
conditions, so it's sort

22:45.576 --> 22:47.176 align:middle
of the essence of what we do.

22:48.276 --> 22:49.046 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

22:50.946 --> 22:52.236 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

22:52.336 --> 22:54.906 align:middle
I just wanted to
ask, first of all,

22:54.906 --> 22:58.536 align:middle
about whether the Fed is
gathering information about some

22:58.596 --> 23:00.706 align:middle
of the new money laundering
information that's come

23:00.706 --> 23:02.586 align:middle
out about Deutsche
Bank and Danske Bank

23:02.586 --> 23:04.176 align:middle
and whether you have
any concerns there?

23:04.276 --> 23:06.316 align:middle
And then, also, I
was just wondering

23:06.316 --> 23:08.796 align:middle
if you could say whether
there's any kind of timeline

23:08.796 --> 23:13.096 align:middle
on when the Fed might make a
decision on real-time payments

23:13.096 --> 23:14.066 align:middle
and whether you're going

23:14.066 --> 23:15.346 align:middle
to build a real-time
payment system?

23:16.056 --> 23:16.566 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

23:16.566 --> 23:19.936 align:middle
Right. So, on the first
question, I can-we don't comment

23:20.326 --> 23:24.956 align:middle
on individual investigations
or whether they're happening.

23:25.126 --> 23:30.006 align:middle
I will say that we take
our enforcement powers very

23:30.006 --> 23:33.496 align:middle
seriously, and we
put them to work

23:33.496 --> 23:34.586 align:middle
when we feel it's justified.

23:35.736 --> 23:37.286 align:middle
In terms of real-time payments,

23:37.346 --> 23:41.396 align:middle
it's something we're
very actively working on.

23:41.396 --> 23:44.006 align:middle
As you obviously know, we
have some proposals out,

23:44.006 --> 23:45.066 align:middle
and we're considering them.

23:45.466 --> 23:49.306 align:middle
You know, the thing is, it's
very important in that space

23:49.306 --> 23:52.556 align:middle
to consult with the full
range of market participants

23:52.556 --> 23:55.586 align:middle
and interest groups, consumer
groups, and we've done that,

23:55.876 --> 23:58.426 align:middle
as I'm sure you know,
over a period of years.

23:58.916 --> 24:01.606 align:middle
We don't have plenary
authority to just do things,

24:01.606 --> 24:03.406 align:middle
for the most part,
in the payment space.

24:03.406 --> 24:06.186 align:middle
We've been more of a
convener, bringing industry

24:06.186 --> 24:09.466 align:middle
and the public-public interest
groups-and all those groups

24:09.466 --> 24:10.126 align:middle
around the table.

24:10.126 --> 24:12.426 align:middle
And we've-I think we've
played a constructive role

24:12.426 --> 24:14.406 align:middle
in that respect, and I
believe that will continue.

24:14.796 --> 24:16.176 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

24:19.706 --> 24:21.586 align:middle
Thanks. Jim Puzzanghera
with the L.A. Times.

24:21.586 --> 24:23.846 align:middle
You and your colleagues may
have noticed, President Trump,

24:23.886 --> 24:26.636 align:middle
for the past six months,
has been urging the Fed not

24:26.636 --> 24:28.886 align:middle
to raise rates-to
stop raising rates.

24:29.396 --> 24:32.246 align:middle
How would you respond to
those who would suggest

24:32.246 --> 24:34.606 align:middle
that the Fed just caved to
the president's demands?

24:35.316 --> 24:35.686 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

24:35.686 --> 24:39.266 align:middle
So what we care about-and,
really, the only thing we care

24:39.266 --> 24:44.406 align:middle
about at the Fed-is doing our
job for the American people

24:44.926 --> 24:46.466 align:middle
and using our tools
appropriately.

24:46.646 --> 24:48.926 align:middle
So that's very strongly
our culture.

24:49.346 --> 24:52.006 align:middle
I think anyone who knows
the Fed or who has worked

24:52.006 --> 24:54.226 align:middle
at the Fed would
recognize that description.

24:54.686 --> 24:58.056 align:middle
So we're always going to do what
we think is the right thing.

24:58.056 --> 25:00.136 align:middle
We're never going to take
political considerations

25:00.136 --> 25:03.996 align:middle
into account or discuss
them as part of our work.

25:04.216 --> 25:05.036 align:middle
You know, we're human.

25:05.036 --> 25:05.916 align:middle
We make mistakes.

25:06.006 --> 25:07.256 align:middle
But we're not going
to make mistakes

25:07.256 --> 25:08.466 align:middle
of character or integrity.

25:09.236 --> 25:12.476 align:middle
And I would want the public to
know that, and I would want them

25:12.576 --> 25:13.816 align:middle
to see that in our actions.

25:15.756 --> 25:17.046 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

25:18.726 --> 25:22.486 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business News.

25:22.486 --> 25:23.756 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

25:23.756 --> 25:26.176 align:middle
The long-term federal
funds rate is 2.8 percent.

25:26.176 --> 25:28.596 align:middle
I've talked the last year with
a number of Fed presidents

25:28.596 --> 25:31.066 align:middle
who worry that under 3
percent is not enough

25:31.066 --> 25:32.106 align:middle
to handle the next recession.

25:32.106 --> 25:34.716 align:middle
You say that's your first
tool, that's your primary means

25:34.936 --> 25:37.166 align:middle
of adjusting monetary policy.

25:37.646 --> 25:40.986 align:middle
So with a larger balance
sheet-with a larger balance

25:40.986 --> 25:43.846 align:middle
sheet, could you-how could you
handle that next recession,

25:43.846 --> 25:45.196 align:middle
then, with the combination
of those two?

25:45.676 --> 25:46.916 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

25:47.086 --> 25:50.306 align:middle
I guess the sense of your
question is that we could be

25:50.306 --> 25:53.026 align:middle
in a situation in the future-we
hope not, but-we could be

25:53.026 --> 25:56.306 align:middle
in a situation where we'd
like to cut rates more

25:56.306 --> 25:58.796 align:middle
than we can effectively, and
we hit the zero lower bound.

25:58.796 --> 26:02.846 align:middle
We don't think anything
like that is in the cards.

26:03.276 --> 26:04.836 align:middle
Now, there's no reason to
think that it would be.

26:05.266 --> 26:08.516 align:middle
But as we said in today's
release, if that happens,

26:08.516 --> 26:10.136 align:middle
then we'll use the full
range of our tools,

26:10.476 --> 26:12.896 align:middle
and that includes
the balance sheet.

26:13.096 --> 26:15.876 align:middle
But we would use it after
using our conventional tools,

26:15.876 --> 26:18.816 align:middle
which would be the interest
rate and forward guidance

26:18.816 --> 26:19.606 align:middle
about the interest rate.

26:19.806 --> 26:20.166 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:20.166 --> 26:24.186 align:middle
Even if you have a large
balance sheet-$4 trillion?

26:24.186 --> 26:25.476 align:middle
Above $4 trillion?

26:25.476 --> 26:26.606 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

26:26.606 --> 26:29.396 align:middle
Yes. There would be room
to do substantially more.

26:33.946 --> 26:35.306 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

26:35.306 --> 26:37.686 align:middle
Chairman Powell,
Donna Borak with CNN.

26:37.686 --> 26:39.756 align:middle
I just wanted to go back
to-you had mentioned

26:39.756 --> 26:42.256 align:middle
on the crosscurrents, the
government shutdown issue.

26:43.036 --> 26:44.576 align:middle
You mentioned a couple
of weeks ago

26:44.576 --> 26:48.526 align:middle
that that information would
clearly show up in the data.

26:48.526 --> 26:51.876 align:middle
I'm wondering, as you guys
discussed during this week's

26:51.876 --> 26:54.906 align:middle
meeting, what the potential
economic impact would be

26:54.906 --> 26:58.056 align:middle
of the shutdown, the possibility
of a second one, whether

26:58.056 --> 27:00.046 align:middle
or not it would diminish
consumer confidence,

27:00.046 --> 27:02.656 align:middle
have some severe ripple effects?

27:03.156 --> 27:05.306 align:middle
Just talking to other
folks outside,

27:06.076 --> 27:08.086 align:middle
businesses-clearly
they were suffering

27:08.086 --> 27:09.156 align:middle
and seeing a lot of pain.

27:09.416 --> 27:10.956 align:middle
I'm just wondering about,
for American families

27:10.956 --> 27:13.746 align:middle
that missed two paychecks,
how is this going to play

27:13.746 --> 27:14.976 align:middle
out for them for
the months to come?

27:15.236 --> 27:15.996 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

27:15.996 --> 27:17.826 align:middle
Right. So let me give
you the economic side

27:17.826 --> 27:22.046 align:middle
of it first-is that, even
with a fairly long shutdown,

27:22.286 --> 27:23.656 align:middle
as long as that's the end of it

27:23.906 --> 27:27.176 align:middle
and everyone gets their
backpay-except the contractors,

27:27.756 --> 27:29.406 align:middle
I guess, the private-sector
contractors,

27:29.406 --> 27:31.156 align:middle
but-some people get
their backpay,

27:31.156 --> 27:37.146 align:middle
then the lost GDP will be
regained in the second quarter.

27:37.966 --> 27:40.086 align:middle
So that's that.

27:40.086 --> 27:42.596 align:middle
I would say that the-you
know, we all see the suffering

27:42.596 --> 27:45.286 align:middle
and the heartbreak and all
the pain people go through.

27:45.286 --> 27:49.216 align:middle
I see, you know, federal
employees doing their work,

27:49.216 --> 27:51.776 align:middle
and I'm, you know,
really grateful they did.

27:51.776 --> 27:53.946 align:middle
I mean, to be doing your work
while you're not getting paid,

27:53.946 --> 27:55.316 align:middle
it's something we should
all be grateful for.

27:55.316 --> 27:58.136 align:middle
Did that answer both
your questions?

27:58.136 --> 27:58.636 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

27:59.816 --> 28:00.356 align:middle
Yes, it does.

28:00.916 --> 28:03.106 align:middle
So you don't see a permanent
effect of this shutdown?

28:03.106 --> 28:06.546 align:middle
And I'm just wondering if
you're thinking about the path

28:07.156 --> 28:08.436 align:middle
of future policy
and the possibility

28:08.436 --> 28:12.316 align:middle
of reaching this point
again on February 15?

28:12.316 --> 28:12.766 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

28:12.766 --> 28:13.886 align:middle
So would there be
a permanent effect?

28:13.886 --> 28:16.206 align:middle
As I mentioned, there
wouldn't be

28:16.206 --> 28:17.506 align:middle
if there isn't another shutdown.

28:18.016 --> 28:19.656 align:middle
If there were going to
be a permanent effect

28:19.786 --> 28:21.646 align:middle
or a lasting effect, let's say,

28:22.016 --> 28:23.546 align:middle
it would be from
a longer shutdown

28:23.546 --> 28:25.556 align:middle
or perhaps a second
shutdown, and that would be

28:26.196 --> 28:30.056 align:middle
through the channel of a loss
of confidence in our ability

28:30.056 --> 28:31.506 align:middle
to make policy in
the United States.

28:31.506 --> 28:32.866 align:middle
That would be the channel.

28:32.866 --> 28:35.186 align:middle
And I think that was
something that we

28:35.186 --> 28:38.336 align:middle
and many others were worried
about as there was talk

28:38.336 --> 28:39.756 align:middle
of an even longer shutdown.

28:40.746 --> 28:43.096 align:middle
In terms of ideas for not
having more shutdowns,

28:43.096 --> 28:44.916 align:middle
I know Congress is actually
looking at some of those,

28:44.916 --> 28:47.476 align:middle
so I think that could be a
profitable thing to explore.

28:49.416 --> 28:50.376 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

28:53.936 --> 28:55.436 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger, AP.

28:55.476 --> 28:57.386 align:middle
The Beige Book that was prepared

28:57.386 --> 29:02.346 align:middle
for this meeting noted a rising
concern among business contacts

29:02.346 --> 29:05.886 align:middle
about higher trade tariffs
and the tensions there.

29:06.186 --> 29:09.696 align:middle
Did that play a part in the
discussion and the decision

29:09.776 --> 29:12.546 align:middle
that you made on shifting the
wording in your statement?

29:12.546 --> 29:14.246 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

29:14.306 --> 29:17.396 align:middle
So, trade-you sound like
you're an avid reader

29:17.396 --> 29:21.396 align:middle
of the Beige Book, and
that's great-the trade,

29:21.396 --> 29:24.106 align:middle
as you will know, then-trade
has been a big feature

29:24.106 --> 29:26.106 align:middle
in the Beige Book
for some time now.

29:26.596 --> 29:30.196 align:middle
The Beige Book is a collection
of all of our-of the comments

29:30.196 --> 29:32.626 align:middle
that our Reserve Bank presidents
get from their Districts.

29:32.626 --> 29:33.786 align:middle
It's incredibly valuable,

29:33.786 --> 29:35.656 align:middle
because you're getting
actual reports

29:35.656 --> 29:37.136 align:middle
from people who are on the line.

29:37.136 --> 29:38.796 align:middle
And there have been
real concerns

29:38.796 --> 29:40.766 align:middle
about trade right along
the line, both in terms

29:40.766 --> 29:45.676 align:middle
of availability of materials
and costs and retaliation

29:45.676 --> 29:47.206 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

29:47.206 --> 29:49.596 align:middle
So that's been a concern.

29:49.676 --> 29:52.476 align:middle
I would say the longer-term
concern, though,

29:52.476 --> 29:56.726 align:middle
is the negotiations
that are going on.

29:57.106 --> 30:01.156 align:middle
If they linger, then there could
be more and more uncertainty,

30:01.156 --> 30:04.606 align:middle
and you worry over time that
that could have an effect

30:04.606 --> 30:05.536 align:middle
on business confidence.

30:05.646 --> 30:07.706 align:middle
So far, the actual
amount of tariffs

30:07.706 --> 30:11.266 align:middle
that have been applied both
here and in China is not enough

30:11.266 --> 30:15.206 align:middle
to have material effects on
GDP either here or in China.

30:15.926 --> 30:19.356 align:middle
So the concern is more a
longer-for me-a longer,

30:19.356 --> 30:22.036 align:middle
drawn-out set of
negotiations back and forth,

30:22.106 --> 30:25.686 align:middle
which could result in
sapping business confidence.

30:25.686 --> 30:28.316 align:middle
Uncertainty is not the
friend of business.

30:28.646 --> 30:32.056 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

30:35.836 --> 30:37.946 align:middle
I'm Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

30:38.006 --> 30:39.866 align:middle
I want to talk to you
about corporate debt.

30:40.296 --> 30:42.646 align:middle
Are you worried that
by taking a pause

30:42.646 --> 30:45.286 align:middle
in raising interest rates-we've
had low interest rates

30:45.346 --> 30:49.306 align:middle
for so long-are you contributing
to a bubble in corporate debt?

30:50.256 --> 30:51.056 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

30:51.316 --> 30:56.626 align:middle
So, we did-we've called out
corporate debt as a risk-more

30:56.626 --> 30:58.306 align:middle
of a macroeconomic
risk, I think,

30:58.306 --> 31:02.426 align:middle
than a financial stability
risk, the sense of that being

31:02.426 --> 31:06.066 align:middle
that if you have companies that
are highly levered and we do go

31:06.066 --> 31:09.106 align:middle
into a downturn, they're going
to be less able to weather that

31:09.106 --> 31:12.226 align:middle
and keep serving their
customers and, you know,

31:12.226 --> 31:14.606 align:middle
may have to do layoffs
and things like that.

31:14.836 --> 31:17.966 align:middle
So they can amplify, in
effect, a negative downturn.

31:18.366 --> 31:19.086 align:middle
So we watch that.

31:19.086 --> 31:21.246 align:middle
We also watch carefully
for the exposure

31:21.246 --> 31:23.106 align:middle
of the financial system
to these companies.

31:23.396 --> 31:26.286 align:middle
In other words, banks are
arranging a lot of these loans.

31:26.286 --> 31:28.166 align:middle
The question is, what
is their exposure?

31:28.166 --> 31:30.356 align:middle
Do they retain big
pieces of the loans?

31:30.356 --> 31:33.336 align:middle
Do they have obligations
to underwrite loans

31:33.336 --> 31:34.946 align:middle
which build up in a pipeline?

31:35.206 --> 31:37.696 align:middle
So we monitor those
risks very carefully.

31:38.216 --> 31:40.986 align:middle
And, frankly, the banks
monitor them much better

31:40.986 --> 31:43.956 align:middle
with our support and help than
they did before the crisis.

31:44.366 --> 31:45.846 align:middle
So it's a concern.

31:45.846 --> 31:46.746 align:middle
It's something that
we're watching.

31:46.746 --> 31:49.026 align:middle
Of course, you know,
November, December,

31:49.026 --> 31:51.106 align:middle
and January were
much slower months

31:51.106 --> 31:52.096 align:middle
for those sorts of things.

31:52.186 --> 31:54.506 align:middle
But, you know, it'll
be something

31:54.506 --> 31:58.976 align:middle
that we're always paying
close attention to.

31:59.176 --> 32:01.596 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

32:02.766 --> 32:04.806 align:middle
Chairman Powell, Greg
Robb from MarketWatch.

32:05.746 --> 32:09.976 align:middle
Financial markets have reacted
strongly to the decision

32:09.976 --> 32:10.896 align:middle
and the press conference.

32:10.996 --> 32:12.586 align:middle
The Dow Jones industrial
average is

32:12.586 --> 32:14.086 align:middle
up more than, like, 500 points.

32:15.316 --> 32:19.196 align:middle
There's a sense in the market
that there's, you know,

32:19.196 --> 32:21.656 align:middle
what people call the
"Powell put" on the markets.

32:22.196 --> 32:24.316 align:middle
Are financial markets
wrong in that assessment?

32:24.316 --> 32:24.656 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

32:26.186 --> 32:29.916 align:middle
You know, I would point to
all the continuity here.

32:30.236 --> 32:32.306 align:middle
You know, the thing we did

32:32.306 --> 32:34.826 align:middle
on the balance sheet is
something we've been working

32:34.826 --> 32:38.416 align:middle
on for, frankly, years-the
thing we announced today.

32:38.416 --> 32:41.166 align:middle
And so we're providing
clarity there.

32:41.166 --> 32:43.526 align:middle
I think that's a
constructive thing to do.

32:43.526 --> 32:46.936 align:middle
My-honestly-only motivation
is to do the right thing

32:47.066 --> 32:49.226 align:middle
for the economy and for the
American people-that's it.

32:49.866 --> 32:51.976 align:middle
And this situation, I think,

32:51.976 --> 32:53.896 align:middle
calls for patience-I
think it does.

32:53.896 --> 32:58.506 align:middle
And it's just the stance of
policy we think is appropriate.

32:59.246 --> 33:02.906 align:middle
We see these uncertainties,
and we see a time

33:02.906 --> 33:06.356 align:middle
when we can afford to-we have
the luxury of being able to wait

33:06.356 --> 33:07.866 align:middle
and watch, and that's
what we're planning to do.

33:07.866 --> 33:09.126 align:middle
And I think it's
the right thing.

33:09.636 --> 33:15.476 align:middle
I feel strongly that it is.

33:15.476 --> 33:16.956 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

33:17.876 --> 33:21.046 align:middle
Chairman Powell, the
Office of the Comptroller

33:21.046 --> 33:25.986 align:middle
of the Currency last year
completed its fintech charter.

33:26.416 --> 33:28.506 align:middle
And one of the questions

33:28.506 --> 33:32.686 align:middle
around that charter has been
whether the Federal Reserve will

33:32.996 --> 33:36.976 align:middle
allow such a chartered bank
to access the payment system.

33:37.476 --> 33:41.416 align:middle
I'm curious if you think a
fintech charter is a good idea

33:41.746 --> 33:44.556 align:middle
in general, and what conditions
you think might be necessary

33:44.556 --> 33:48.166 align:middle
to allow the Fed to allow such
a chartered institution access

33:48.256 --> 33:49.626 align:middle
to the payment system.

33:49.876 --> 33:50.246 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

33:50.246 --> 33:51.786 align:middle
I'm not going to be able
to help you much on that.

33:51.786 --> 33:54.416 align:middle
Those are great questions, and
those are questions that are,

33:54.416 --> 33:58.166 align:middle
you know, that our great
supervisory people are

33:58.166 --> 33:58.946 align:middle
looking at.

33:58.946 --> 34:05.246 align:middle
I think we're open to these
ideas, but I don't have any news

34:05.246 --> 34:06.056 align:middle
for you on that today.

34:07.116 --> 34:08.506 align:middle
STEVE BECKNER.

34:09.046 --> 34:12.386 align:middle
Thank you.

34:13.296 --> 34:17.636 align:middle
Steve Beckner, freelance
journalist reporting for NPR.

34:17.636 --> 34:21.136 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, you've echoed
the widespread concern

34:21.136 --> 34:24.726 align:middle
about slowing growth in China
and elsewhere in the world,

34:25.086 --> 34:27.946 align:middle
but I wonder whether
it's possible

34:27.946 --> 34:31.596 align:middle
that slower growth abroad
might actually be beneficial

34:31.596 --> 34:36.056 align:middle
to this country, in some
respects, at least-for example,

34:36.056 --> 34:39.466 align:middle
perhaps increasing
capital inflows

34:39.466 --> 34:44.436 align:middle
and putting downward pressure on
long-term rates in this country,

34:44.856 --> 34:46.936 align:middle
as happened in the
Asian financial crisis

34:46.936 --> 34:47.956 align:middle
in the late '90s.

34:49.526 --> 34:49.966 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

34:50.306 --> 34:51.896 align:middle
You know, Steve, it's
certainly possible.

34:53.756 --> 34:56.886 align:middle
Ultimately, though, you know,

34:56.886 --> 34:59.106 align:middle
a strong global economy
is good for us.

34:59.196 --> 35:00.646 align:middle
We export to these countries.

35:00.836 --> 35:02.026 align:middle
We trade with these countries.

35:02.676 --> 35:05.976 align:middle
I mean, part of the story
of 2017, for example,

35:05.976 --> 35:10.026 align:middle
was that European growth kept
coming in stronger and stronger,

35:10.026 --> 35:11.456 align:middle
and that meant the
euro was strong,

35:11.586 --> 35:13.406 align:middle
and that supported our exports.

35:13.966 --> 35:15.976 align:middle
So, ultimately, I
think we benefit

35:15.976 --> 35:19.296 align:middle
from having strong growth
abroad, although you point

35:19.296 --> 35:20.076 align:middle
to an interesting case.

35:21.396 --> 35:22.746 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

35:27.146 --> 35:29.046 align:middle
Chairman Powell, Mark
Hamrick with Bankrate.

35:29.046 --> 35:29.276 align:middle
Thank you.

35:29.276 --> 35:30.966 align:middle
As you probably heard,

35:30.966 --> 35:32.916 align:middle
the Congressional Budget
Office is projecting

35:32.916 --> 35:37.866 align:middle
that federal debt is expected
to surge to about $29 trillion

35:37.866 --> 35:40.796 align:middle
over the next 10 years,
reaching the highest share

35:40.796 --> 35:42.246 align:middle
since the end of World War II.

35:42.246 --> 35:43.316 align:middle
First of all, does that sound

35:43.316 --> 35:45.466 align:middle
like it could be a
reasonable forecast,

35:45.996 --> 35:49.236 align:middle
with the Administration seeming
to take plenty of opportunities

35:49.236 --> 35:52.436 align:middle
to question the legitimacy
of CBO?

35:52.436 --> 35:55.296 align:middle
And then, secondly, what effect
does that have on interest rates

35:55.296 --> 35:57.936 align:middle
and the public if indeed that's
even close to being true?

35:58.896 --> 35:59.736 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

35:59.736 --> 36:06.796 align:middle
So, first, it is important-we
don't do fiscal policy.

36:06.796 --> 36:09.316 align:middle
We don't advise the government
or Congress on fiscal policy,

36:09.316 --> 36:13.096 align:middle
so I'll limit myself to more
high-level comments and say

36:13.096 --> 36:18.416 align:middle
that it is not a secret-it
is a long-known fact

36:18.416 --> 36:20.896 align:middle
that the U.S. federal
government budget is

36:20.896 --> 36:24.346 align:middle
on an unsustainable path, and
that needs to be addressed.

36:25.066 --> 36:28.836 align:middle
And that is driven
principally by the combination

36:28.836 --> 36:31.146 align:middle
of health-care costs-really,

36:31.146 --> 36:34.656 align:middle
due to our health-care
delivery system-and the aging

36:34.656 --> 36:35.306 align:middle
of the population.

36:35.506 --> 36:40.076 align:middle
And there's no time like
now-when the economy is healthy,

36:40.496 --> 36:45.116 align:middle
growing, people are working-to
go after that problem.

36:45.306 --> 36:48.096 align:middle
Ultimately, we will have to.

36:48.736 --> 36:49.706 align:middle
We don't have a forecast.

36:49.706 --> 36:51.206 align:middle
We don't forecast those things.

36:51.376 --> 36:54.046 align:middle
I have no reason to doubt
CBO's numbers on this.

36:54.046 --> 36:55.496 align:middle
I haven't actually
looked at that report,

36:55.876 --> 36:58.816 align:middle
but my general experience
with them is

36:58.816 --> 36:59.946 align:middle
that it's a professional outfit.

37:00.786 --> 37:01.516 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

37:01.566 --> 37:02.786 align:middle
What does that look like,

37:02.786 --> 37:06.756 align:middle
if that really takes place
10 years down the road?

37:06.756 --> 37:07.386 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

37:07.386 --> 37:10.066 align:middle
So, you know, first
of all, our-the work

37:10.066 --> 37:11.686 align:middle
that we do relates more

37:11.686 --> 37:14.796 align:middle
to the medium term
rather than the long term.

37:15.146 --> 37:18.536 align:middle
And so I don't see
this, in particular,

37:18.586 --> 37:21.306 align:middle
as a threat to this
business cycle

37:21.306 --> 37:23.126 align:middle
or to the economy this year.

37:23.186 --> 37:26.256 align:middle
It's more in the longer
run, we'll be spending all

37:26.256 --> 37:29.716 align:middle
of our money on paying
interest and not on the things

37:29.716 --> 37:32.136 align:middle
that we really need to be
doing for future generations

37:32.956 --> 37:33.986 align:middle
and for our own generation.

37:34.576 --> 37:37.036 align:middle
So it's a serious problem,
but it's not a problem

37:37.036 --> 37:40.816 align:middle
that is something that either
helps or prevents the Fed

37:40.876 --> 37:42.496 align:middle
from doing our job today.

37:43.026 --> 37:48.016 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

37:48.086 --> 37:48.526 align:middle
Hi, Chairman.

37:48.526 --> 37:50.926 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

37:51.366 --> 37:54.906 align:middle
I know where the FOMC
participants' estimates

37:54.906 --> 37:57.976 align:middle
for the long-run
interest rate are.

37:58.316 --> 38:00.666 align:middle
They're all above 2½ percent.

38:01.196 --> 38:04.886 align:middle
Right now we're between
2.25 and 2½.

38:05.396 --> 38:10.816 align:middle
So I think a key
question right now is,

38:10.816 --> 38:13.956 align:middle
is policy accommodative
at this moment?

38:14.386 --> 38:18.706 align:middle
Are we looking at staying
accommodative for some time,

38:18.706 --> 38:25.396 align:middle
or has your estimate
of neutral come down,

38:25.396 --> 38:27.796 align:middle
in light of the recent
volatility in markets?

38:28.046 --> 38:29.276 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

38:29.276 --> 38:32.986 align:middle
So the range of estimates on the
Committee starts at 2½ percent,

38:32.986 --> 38:35.246 align:middle
and that's kind of roughly
where we currently are.

38:35.326 --> 38:39.426 align:middle
And as I've said a couple
of times, when you get

38:39.426 --> 38:42.776 align:middle
to that range-you know that
we can't directly observe the

38:42.776 --> 38:43.306 align:middle
neutral rate.

38:43.306 --> 38:44.676 align:middle
We only know it by its works.

38:44.846 --> 38:49.236 align:middle
And so we have to put aside
our own priors of what

38:49.236 --> 38:51.666 align:middle
that rate might be and
let the data speak to us.

38:51.796 --> 38:53.326 align:middle
So we can do that.

38:53.326 --> 38:54.726 align:middle
We're in the range.

38:54.806 --> 38:57.296 align:middle
There are a number of Committee
members who are, you know,

38:57.296 --> 38:58.316 align:middle
right around that range.

38:58.376 --> 39:01.586 align:middle
And I think we're
watching to see.

39:01.706 --> 39:05.416 align:middle
We don't-I don't-again, I think

39:05.416 --> 39:08.286 align:middle
that our policy stance
today is appropriate

39:08.286 --> 39:09.316 align:middle
for the state of the economy.

39:09.366 --> 39:10.456 align:middle
That's my feeling.

39:10.456 --> 39:13.736 align:middle
We're going to be watching data
to see whether that's right,

39:13.736 --> 39:14.946 align:middle
and we're also going
to be watching data

39:14.946 --> 39:17.616 align:middle
to see how these
crosscurrents resolve themselves

39:17.616 --> 39:21.546 align:middle
and how the U.S. economy
performs this year.

39:21.546 --> 39:22.196 align:middle
VIRGINIE MONTET.

39:25.046 --> 39:25.556 align:middle
Thank you.

39:25.556 --> 39:27.796 align:middle
Virginie Montet with
Agence France-Presse.

39:28.536 --> 39:32.246 align:middle
Among the global developments
that the Fed is monitoring,

39:32.446 --> 39:36.786 align:middle
how much of a risk for the
American expansion would be the

39:36.786 --> 39:38.516 align:middle
prospect of a "hard" Brexit?

39:39.126 --> 39:41.686 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

39:41.796 --> 39:47.696 align:middle
So we've been monitoring the
Brexit situation very carefully

39:47.696 --> 39:48.466 align:middle
for a long time.

39:48.696 --> 39:52.256 align:middle
And for us, that starts with
U.S. financial institutions

39:52.256 --> 39:56.756 align:middle
that have a presence either in
the U.K. or in the EU or both.

39:57.346 --> 40:01.306 align:middle
So we have worked with those
institutions alongside U.K.

40:01.306 --> 40:03.886 align:middle
and EU regulators
to assure ourselves

40:03.886 --> 40:08.886 align:middle
that those firms have plans
and have liquidity and have all

40:08.886 --> 40:10.476 align:middle
of the things they
will need to deal

40:10.476 --> 40:12.666 align:middle
with the full range
of Brexit outcomes.

40:12.666 --> 40:15.506 align:middle
So we've done a lot
of work on that, and,

40:15.506 --> 40:19.876 align:middle
generally speaking-again, a lot
of work has been done-we have

40:19.876 --> 40:23.886 align:middle
to be humble and say that this
is an unprecedented event.

40:23.886 --> 40:29.066 align:middle
But that's the financial
system aspect of it.

40:29.856 --> 40:32.326 align:middle
If there is a hard Brexit,

40:32.576 --> 40:36.546 align:middle
then that would very likely
involve disruptions both

40:36.546 --> 40:39.096 align:middle
to the continental economy and
certainly to the U.K. economy,

40:39.826 --> 40:41.876 align:middle
and we would feel that.

40:42.166 --> 40:45.606 align:middle
The question for us-it wouldn't
be a huge first-order thing,

40:45.606 --> 40:50.506 align:middle
the economic effects, unless
you saw financial disruptions.

40:50.506 --> 40:53.976 align:middle
So, if you saw financial
turmoil and that kind of thing,

40:53.976 --> 40:55.316 align:middle
that would be the way
it would reach us.

40:55.376 --> 40:58.596 align:middle
I would expect that we
would feel some of this,

40:58.596 --> 41:01.996 align:middle
and it's very hard to
have great confidence

41:01.996 --> 41:04.466 align:middle
that you know what
that would be.

41:04.546 --> 41:08.956 align:middle
But it would be
something-probably not material

41:09.006 --> 41:11.016 align:middle
to our economy, but it's
something we'll be watching very

41:11.016 --> 41:12.546 align:middle
carefully and certainly hoping

41:12.546 --> 41:16.796 align:middle
that there is a resolution
short of a hard Brexit.

41:17.616 --> 41:18.326 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

41:19.936 --> 41:21.946 align:middle
Hi, I'm Jean Yung
with Market News.

41:22.426 --> 41:24.476 align:middle
I have a question
about the ultimate size

41:24.476 --> 41:25.346 align:middle
of the balance sheet.

41:25.756 --> 41:29.406 align:middle
How will the Fed judge
what is a reasonable level

41:29.406 --> 41:32.696 align:middle
for financial institutions'
demand for reserves

41:33.596 --> 41:37.046 align:middle
when it appears to be rising at
a fast pace over the past year

41:37.376 --> 41:39.956 align:middle
and perhaps at a faster
pace than can be explained

41:39.956 --> 41:43.336 align:middle
by regulatory changes
during that same time frame?

41:43.696 --> 41:46.846 align:middle
Would you prefer to err on the
side of being more generous,

41:47.306 --> 41:49.106 align:middle
or would you try
to encourage banks

41:49.266 --> 41:51.316 align:middle
from holding-to hold
less reserves?

41:51.316 --> 41:51.636 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

41:51.986 --> 41:53.206 align:middle
Let me be clear.

41:53.206 --> 41:58.276 align:middle
I don't know that demand

41:58.276 --> 42:00.246 align:middle
for reserves has risen
over the past years.

42:00.246 --> 42:03.606 align:middle
I think our understanding of
demand for reserves-remember

42:03.606 --> 42:08.226 align:middle
that the banks have more
reserves than they need.

42:08.276 --> 42:10.306 align:middle
Reserves are still
quite abundant.

42:10.306 --> 42:14.766 align:middle
So the question is, how much of
that amount is actually going

42:14.766 --> 42:19.306 align:middle
to be needed in the end after,
you know, after the firms adjust

42:19.306 --> 42:21.796 align:middle
to our very gradual decrease?

42:22.096 --> 42:24.286 align:middle
And so our understanding,
really, of the distribution

42:24.286 --> 42:26.546 align:middle
of reserves and how much
will be needed has moved

42:26.546 --> 42:27.906 align:middle
up over the past year.

42:28.466 --> 42:30.596 align:middle
And then there would be
a buffer on top of that.

42:30.596 --> 42:34.716 align:middle
And then we would want to be-we
would want to have a buffer,

42:34.716 --> 42:38.566 align:middle
as I mentioned in my remarks,
because we want to be operating

42:38.566 --> 42:41.196 align:middle
in an abundant reserves
regime where we operate

42:41.196 --> 42:42.386 align:middle
through our administered rates.

42:42.776 --> 42:46.516 align:middle
If you operate too close to that
point of scarcity, then you wind

42:46.516 --> 42:48.216 align:middle
up having to have these big,

42:48.256 --> 42:50.166 align:middle
ongoing interventions
in the market.

42:50.486 --> 42:53.106 align:middle
We don't want the Fed
to have a, you know,

42:53.106 --> 42:55.956 align:middle
a large ongoing presence
in the market around this.

42:55.956 --> 42:59.586 align:middle
We'd rather just, you know-in
managing the federal funds rate,

42:59.946 --> 43:02.056 align:middle
we'd rather have it set
by our administered rates.

43:02.056 --> 43:05.346 align:middle
So that implies you'd want
to be a bit above what

43:05.346 --> 43:07.306 align:middle
that equilibrium
demand for reserves is.

43:07.746 --> 43:10.446 align:middle
And, again, there's no cookbook
here, there's no playbook.

43:10.446 --> 43:11.456 align:middle
No one really knows.

43:11.456 --> 43:14.956 align:middle
The only way you can figure
it out is by surveying people

43:14.956 --> 43:18.076 align:middle
and market intelligence and
then, ultimately, by approaching

43:18.076 --> 43:19.866 align:middle
that point quite carefully.

43:21.076 --> 43:21.486 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

43:21.656 --> 43:26.606 align:middle
But do you take the survey
results as given, or do you try

43:26.896 --> 43:29.626 align:middle
to encourage banks from
holding a [inaudible]?

43:29.626 --> 43:30.226 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

43:30.226 --> 43:32.166 align:middle
We don't take-you know, we
don't think we have a precise

43:32.166 --> 43:33.266 align:middle
understanding of this at all.

43:33.266 --> 43:34.366 align:middle
I want to be clear about that.

43:34.366 --> 43:36.326 align:middle
These estimates are
fairly uncertain.

43:36.326 --> 43:41.196 align:middle
So if you think that
the level of demand

43:41.196 --> 43:42.976 align:middle
for reserves is here
[gesturing with hands together,

43:43.076 --> 43:46.046 align:middle
in the middle], all you can
say is, it's probably somewhere

43:46.046 --> 43:46.566 align:middle
in this range [gesturing
with hands apart,

43:46.566 --> 43:47.236 align:middle
equidistant from the middle].

43:47.236 --> 43:50.016 align:middle
If banks want to use
reserves for a good

43:50.016 --> 43:52.936 align:middle
and sufficient reason-which
is to say, to hold liquidity

43:53.076 --> 43:55.846 align:middle
as we require them to do to meet
the liquidity coverage ratio,

43:55.846 --> 43:57.276 align:middle
to meet their resolution
requirements

43:57.276 --> 43:59.016 align:middle
and resolution planning-we're
not going

43:59.016 --> 44:00.496 align:middle
to discourage them
from holding them.

44:01.216 --> 44:03.456 align:middle
They are, in fact, a
very safe asset-in a way,

44:03.456 --> 44:04.476 align:middle
the safest asset.

44:04.626 --> 44:06.196 align:middle
And we want the banks
to be safe and sound.

44:06.636 --> 44:09.556 align:middle
We didn't create the reserves,
you know, with that in mind,

44:09.556 --> 44:13.146 align:middle
but, you know, since-in
the post-crisis, you know,

44:13.416 --> 44:15.956 align:middle
regulatory regime,
we have brand-new

44:15.956 --> 44:18.596 align:middle
and quite substantial
liquidity requirements,

44:18.596 --> 44:22.076 align:middle
which are very good and which
have great public benefit.

44:22.826 --> 44:25.356 align:middle
So it's not a bad thing that
banks hold onto these reserves

44:25.356 --> 44:26.616 align:middle
or another safe asset.

44:26.616 --> 44:28.806 align:middle
We don't-we're not
encouraging them

44:28.806 --> 44:30.776 align:middle
to hold reserves
instead of Treasuries.

44:31.036 --> 44:34.026 align:middle
Treasuries or reserves
are roughly equivalent

44:34.736 --> 44:36.026 align:middle
for this purpose.

44:36.576 --> 44:37.546 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

44:40.366 --> 44:41.136 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

44:41.296 --> 44:42.816 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

44:42.816 --> 44:47.206 align:middle
A data-dependency question:
Has there been a prioritization

44:47.296 --> 44:51.436 align:middle
of market data over
the economic data?

44:51.616 --> 44:53.196 align:middle
How do you balance
those two things?

44:53.406 --> 44:57.516 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

44:57.586 --> 45:02.486 align:middle
I would say that, you know, our
mandate is maximum employment

45:02.486 --> 45:05.296 align:middle
and stable prices, and
that's about, you know, hard,

45:05.446 --> 45:08.556 align:middle
real-side economic data.

45:09.406 --> 45:14.136 align:middle
We-as I mentioned earlier,
we-our tool, you know,

45:14.136 --> 45:17.346 align:middle
our interest rate tool
operates on the economy

45:17.346 --> 45:18.536 align:middle
through financial conditions.

45:18.536 --> 45:21.606 align:middle
So financial conditions matter,
and they matter in the way

45:21.606 --> 45:23.366 align:middle
that I suggested
earlier-which is to say,

45:23.766 --> 45:26.566 align:middle
broad financial conditions
changing

45:26.626 --> 45:29.176 align:middle
over a sustained
period-that has implications

45:29.176 --> 45:30.106 align:middle
for the macroeconomy.

45:30.106 --> 45:31.886 align:middle
It does. So if you
lower interest rates

45:31.886 --> 45:34.596 align:middle
and they stay low,
every borrower

45:34.706 --> 45:36.706 align:middle
in the country ultimately
has a lower interest rate.

45:36.706 --> 45:42.936 align:middle
That'll have an effect on-over
time, an effect on the economy.

45:43.006 --> 45:47.786 align:middle
But, again, the entire focus we
have is on maximum employment

45:47.846 --> 45:51.006 align:middle
and stable prices, not on any
particular financial market

45:51.006 --> 45:53.446 align:middle
or financial conditions
generally.

45:54.316 --> 45:55.056 align:middle
MYLES UDLAND.

45:55.616 --> 46:01.246 align:middle
Chairman Powell, Myles
Udland with Yahoo Finance.

46:01.246 --> 46:04.326 align:middle
I would just ask, I guess, about
the balance sheet in general.

46:04.326 --> 46:06.156 align:middle
And are you surprised
at how much

46:06.156 --> 46:08.996 align:middle
of a conversation we're having
around the balance sheet,

46:08.996 --> 46:11.466 align:middle
and how much talk about the
balance sheet from yourself

46:11.466 --> 46:14.406 align:middle
and various Fed officials
have moved financial markets?

46:14.406 --> 46:16.826 align:middle
Because putting out a
statement, clarifying your view

46:16.826 --> 46:19.806 align:middle
on the balance sheet, you
know, is somewhat surprising,

46:19.806 --> 46:21.676 align:middle
considering it is
restating, you know,

46:21.676 --> 46:23.716 align:middle
what the Fed had said
all along was the goal

46:23.716 --> 46:26.756 align:middle
with balance sheet
normalization-to roll it off,

46:26.756 --> 46:28.436 align:middle
not have it be a
key part of policy.

46:28.436 --> 46:31.686 align:middle
And so I would just ask,
are you surprised by how far

46:31.686 --> 46:34.066 align:middle
that conversation has gotten
just in the last six weeks?

46:34.526 --> 46:35.036 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

46:35.036 --> 46:37.456 align:middle
You know, I'll quickly
go back-in 2017,

46:37.456 --> 46:39.776 align:middle
we were-in designing
the normalization plan,

46:40.346 --> 46:43.756 align:middle
we were concerned at not having
two active tools of policy.

46:43.756 --> 46:45.936 align:middle
We learned during the
taper tantrum, frankly,

46:45.936 --> 46:49.126 align:middle
in 2013 that was-that would
be confusing to markets.

46:49.126 --> 46:53.816 align:middle
So what we did was we
set up the normalization

46:53.816 --> 46:56.696 align:middle
of the balance sheet in a
way that was very transparent

46:56.696 --> 46:59.096 align:middle
so that you could
look and know really,

46:59.416 --> 47:03.046 align:middle
certainly as to Treasuries,
pretty much the exact amounts

47:03.046 --> 47:05.746 align:middle
and the timing in which we
would be returning these assets

47:05.746 --> 47:06.716 align:middle
to private hands.

47:07.226 --> 47:10.266 align:middle
And we put that out there
very publicly and in the hope

47:10.266 --> 47:11.996 align:middle
that it would be priced
in and understood,

47:12.666 --> 47:15.736 align:middle
and that then we could put
the balance sheet on the side

47:16.066 --> 47:19.036 align:middle
and have interest rates be
the active tool of policy.

47:19.916 --> 47:23.856 align:middle
So that went along that way very
well, and I think that division

47:23.856 --> 47:26.876 align:middle
of labor was a good
one for our policy

47:26.876 --> 47:29.806 align:middle
and for the benefit
of the country.

47:30.106 --> 47:34.636 align:middle
I think that there's-I think
the market is now looking

47:34.636 --> 47:36.476 align:middle
for more clarity around that,

47:36.476 --> 47:38.776 align:middle
and I think we'll
be providing it.

47:38.886 --> 47:39.496 align:middle
That's what's happened.

47:41.346 --> 47:42.046 align:middle
Thanks very much.

